Here's a recommended structure:

**1. Make HTTP calls injectable**

```csharp
// In your MCP server project
public class MyTool
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;

    public MyTool(HttpClient httpClient)
    {
        _httpClient = httpClient;
    }

    public async Task<string> GetDataAsync(string id)
    {
        var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync($"/api/items/{id}");
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
    }
}
```

**2. Create a test project**

```bash
dotnet new xunit -n MyMcpServer.Tests
cd MyMcpServer.Tests
dotnet add reference ../MyMcpServer/MyMcpServer.csproj
```

**3. Use `MockHttpMessageHandler` to fake responses**

```csharp
// TestHelpers/MockHttpMessageHandler.cs
public class MockHttpMessageHandler : HttpMessageHandler
{
    private readonly Func<HttpRequestMessage, Task<HttpResponseMessage>> _handler;

    public MockHttpMessageHandler(Func<HttpRequestMessage, Task<HttpResponseMessage>> handler)
        => _handler = handler;

    protected override Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync(
        HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
        => _handler(request);
}
```

**4. Write tests against the fake**

```csharp
public class MyToolTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task GetDataAsync_ReturnsExpectedPayload()
    {
        // Arrange — fake the external API
        var handler = new MockHttpMessageHandler((req) =>
        {
            Assert.Equal("/api/items/42", req.RequestUri?.PathAndQuery);

            return Task.FromResult(new HttpResponseMessage(HttpStatusCode.OK)
            {
                Content = new StringContent("{\"id\":42,\"name\":\"Widget\"}")
            });
        });

        var httpClient = new HttpClient(handler)
        {
            BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com")
        };

        var tool = new MyTool(httpClient);

        // Act
        var result = await tool.GetDataAsync("42");

        // Assert
        Assert.Contains("Widget", result);
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task GetDataAsync_ThrowsOnServerError()
    {
        var handler = new MockHttpMessageHandler(_ =>
            Task.FromResult(new HttpResponseMessage(HttpStatusCode.InternalServerError)));

        var httpClient = new HttpClient(handler)
        {
            BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com")
        };

        var tool = new MyTool(httpClient);

        await Assert.ThrowsAsync<HttpRequestException>(
            () => tool.GetDataAsync("42"));
    }
}
```

**Key principles:**

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Inject `HttpClient`** | Constructor injection; lets tests swap in fakes |
| **Mock at `HttpMessageHandler`** | No third-party mocking library needed; controls the raw HTTP layer |
| **Test tool logic, not HTTP** | Verify your parsing, error handling, and return values |
| **Project layout** | `MyMcpServer/` (server) + `MyMcpServer.Tests/` (tests) side by side |

If you prefer a library, **[WireMock.Net](https://github.com/WireMock-Net/WireMock.Net)** spins up a local HTTP server for more realistic integration tests, and **Moq** works well if you extract an `IApiClient` interface instead of using `HttpClient` directly.
